(window.STEP_DATA = window.STEP_DATA || {})[29] = {"n":29,"set":"C","level":"Exam Standard","band":"B1 15% · B2 50% · B2+ 35%","sec":[{"id":"R","name":"Reading Comprehension","ar":"فهم المقروء","min":70,"from":1,"to":40,"g":[{"t":"Gravitational waves","p":["On 14 September 2015, two enormous detectors in the United States, one in Louisiana and one in Washington State, recorded a tiny signal lasting just a fraction of a second. The signal was produced by an event that had taken place 1.3 billion years earlier, when two black holes, each about thirty times the mass of the Sun, spiralled towards each other and merged. For the first time, scientists had directly detected gravitational waves, ripples in the fabric of space and time, confirming a prediction Albert Einstein had made a century before.","According to Einstein's general theory of relativity, mass bends space and time, and gravity is the result of this curvature. When massive objects accelerate, for example when two dense stars orbit each other, they create disturbances that travel outwards at the speed of light, rather like the ripples produced when a stone is dropped into a pond. Einstein himself doubted that these waves could ever be detected, because their effects on Earth would be unimaginably small.","He was right about their size. As a gravitational wave passes, it stretches space in one direction and squeezes it in the other, but the change is minute. The waves detected in 2015 altered the length of the detectors' four-kilometre arms by less than one ten-thousandth of the width of a proton. Measuring such a tiny change required one of the most sensitive instruments ever built.","The detectors, known as LIGO, work by splitting a laser beam into two and sending the beams along two long tunnels set at right angles. Mirrors at the end of each tunnel reflect the beams back, where they are recombined. Normally, the beams cancel each other out exactly, but if a gravitational wave changes the length of one arm relative to the other, a faint signal of light appears. To avoid being fooled by vibrations from passing lorries, earthquakes or even ocean waves, the mirrors are suspended from complex systems that isolate them from the ground, and two detectors far apart are used, so that a real signal must appear in both within a few milliseconds.","The discovery, which earned three American physicists the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2017, has opened a new way of studying the universe. Since then, detectors in the United States, Italy and Japan have recorded dozens of events, mostly mergers of black holes. In 2017, scientists detected waves from the collision of two neutron stars, extremely dense remains of dead stars. Within seconds, telescopes around the world turned towards the source and observed a flash of light, providing evidence that such collisions produce heavy elements such as gold and platinum.","Gravitational-wave astronomy is still in its early days. Scientists hope that future detectors, including a proposed space-based observatory called LISA, consisting of three spacecraft millions of kilometres apart, will detect waves from sources that current instruments cannot reach, such as supermassive black holes merging at the centres of galaxies. Some researchers even hope to detect gravitational waves from the earliest moments of the universe. Just as the invention of the telescope transformed astronomy four centuries ago, the ability to 'hear' the universe through gravitational waves may lead to discoveries that no one can yet predict."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the life of Albert Einstein","how lasers are manufactured","earthquakes in the United States","the detection of gravitational waves and its importance"],"k":3,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage focuses on gravitational waves.","h":"موجات الجاذبية."},{"n":2,"s":"What produced the signal detected in 2015?","o":["the merger of two black holes","an earthquake in Louisiana","a laser experiment","the explosion of the Sun"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 1 explains this.","h":"اندماج ثقبين أسودين."},{"n":3,"s":"Why does the writer compare gravitational waves to ripples in a pond?","o":["to show that the waves contain water","to explain why the waves are dangerous","to help readers imagine how disturbances spread outwards","to describe the shape of black holes"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"The analogy illustrates spreading waves.","h":"تشبيه للتوضيح."},{"n":4,"s":"The word 'minute' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["lasting sixty seconds","very important","rapid","extremely small"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"The change is tiny.","h":"ضئيل جدًا."},{"n":5,"s":"Why are two detectors far apart used?","o":["to double the size of the laser","because one detector is always broken","to confirm that a signal is real and not caused by local vibrations","to measure the temperature of space"],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"A real signal must appear in both.","h":"التأكد من صحة الإشارة."},{"n":6,"s":"In paragraph 4, the word 'they' in 'where they are recombined' refers to","o":["the mirrors","the tunnels","the beams","the detectors"],"k":2,"ty":"reference","e":"The beams are recombined.","h":"تعود على الأشعة."},{"n":7,"s":"What did the observation of colliding neutron stars in 2017 provide evidence for?","o":["Black holes do not exist.","Such collisions produce heavy elements like gold.","Light travels faster than gravity.","Neutron stars are very large."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"إنتاج العناصر الثقيلة."},{"n":8,"s":"What can be inferred about LISA?","o":["It has already been launched.","It will replace telescopes.","It could detect sources that current detectors cannot.","It is located in Louisiana."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"It is proposed and aims at new sources.","h":"سيرصد مصادر جديدة."},{"n":9,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a possible source of false signals?","o":["solar radiation","passing lorries","earthquakes","ocean waves"],"k":0,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Solar radiation is not mentioned.","h":"الإشعاع الشمسي غير مذكور."},{"n":10,"s":"What is the writer's attitude towards gravitational-wave astronomy?","o":["excited about its future possibilities","doubtful that it is useful","critical of its cost","uninterested in its results"],"k":0,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"The final paragraph compares it to the telescope.","h":"حماس للمستقبل."}]},{"t":"Bedouin navigation by the stars","p":["For centuries, the Bedouin of the Arabian Peninsula crossed vast deserts where there were few roads, landmarks or reliable maps. A wrong turn could mean missing a well and running out of water, with fatal consequences. To survive, they developed a sophisticated body of knowledge about the natural world, including weather, plants, animals and the location of water sources. Among the most important elements of this knowledge was the ability to navigate by the stars.","Many Bedouin travelled at night, especially in summer, when daytime temperatures made long journeys dangerous for both people and animals. On clear desert nights, without the light pollution of cities, thousands of stars are visible, and experienced guides could recognize dozens of them by name. The most important was the Pole Star, known in Arabic as al-Jadi. Because it lies almost directly above the Earth's North Pole, it appears to stay in the same place throughout the night, while other stars move across the sky. Facing it, a traveller always knew which direction was north.","The Bedouin also used the rising and setting points of particular stars to mark directions, and they observed how the positions of stars changed through the year. Arabic astronomical tradition identified twenty-eight 'lunar mansions', groups of stars through which the Moon passes during its monthly cycle. The appearance of certain stars just before sunrise marked the beginning of seasons and helped people to predict changes in weather. The rising of Suhail, the bright star known in English as Canopus, for example, was traditionally associated with the end of the worst summer heat.","This knowledge was not written down in books by most Bedouin; it was passed orally from one generation to the next, often through poetry, proverbs and stories. Children learned the names of stars by listening to their elders around the fire. Many Arabic star names entered European languages through translations of Arabic astronomical works during the Middle Ages, which is why stars such as Aldebaran, Altair, Deneb and Betelgeuse still have names of Arabic origin.","Stars were not the only guides. Bedouin navigators also read the shape and direction of sand dunes, which are formed by prevailing winds, the colour and texture of the ground, the behaviour of animals and the position of the sun during the day. Expert guides could reportedly identify their location by tasting or smelling the sand. This combination of skills allowed caravans to cross distances of hundreds of kilometres with remarkable accuracy.","Today, satellite navigation has made traditional skills far less necessary, and much of this knowledge is in danger of being lost as older generations pass away. However, there is growing interest in preserving it. Researchers have recorded interviews with elderly Bedouin, and cultural organizations in Saudi Arabia have organized desert camps and astronomy events where young people can learn traditional star names and stories. Such efforts recognize that this knowledge is not only practical but also an important part of the region's cultural heritage, reflecting a deep and careful relationship between people and their environment."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Which of the following is the best title for the passage?","o":["The History of Satellite Navigation","How Sand Dunes Are Formed","Famous Astronomers of Europe","Reading the Sky: Traditional Desert Navigation"],"k":3,"ty":"best title","e":"The passage focuses on Bedouin star navigation.","h":"قراءة السماء."},{"n":12,"s":"Why did many Bedouin travel at night in summer?","o":["The stars were brighter in summer.","Daytime temperatures were dangerous.","Wells were open only at night.","Animals could not see during the day."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"حرارة النهار."},{"n":13,"s":"Why was the Pole Star especially useful for navigation?","o":["It is the brightest star in the sky.","It appears to stay in the same place, showing north.","It rises in the east every night.","It changes colour with the seasons."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"ثابت ويدل على الشمال."},{"n":14,"s":"What was the rising of Suhail traditionally associated with?","o":["the beginning of the rainy season","the start of the new year","the arrival of sandstorms","the end of the worst summer heat"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"انتهاء شدة الحر."},{"n":15,"s":"The word 'orally' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["by speaking","in writing","secretly","quickly"],"k":0,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Passed through speech.","h":"شفهيًا."},{"n":16,"s":"Why do some stars have names of Arabic origin?","o":["Arab travellers named stars in Europe.","Arabic astronomical works were translated in medieval Europe.","European astronomers studied Bedouin poetry.","The stars are visible only in Arabia."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"ترجمة الأعمال الفلكية."},{"n":17,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a guide used by Bedouin navigators?","o":["sand dunes","animal behaviour","the position of the sun","compasses"],"k":3,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Compasses are not mentioned.","h":"البوصلة غير مذكورة."},{"n":18,"s":"What does the writer suggest by saying expert guides could 'reportedly' identify their location by tasting sand?","o":["It is a well-proven scientific fact.","The claim may not be fully verified.","All Bedouin could do this.","The writer has done it personally."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"'reportedly' signals an unverified claim.","h":"ادعاء غير مؤكد."},{"n":19,"s":"In paragraph 6, the word 'it' in 'preserving it' refers to","o":["satellite navigation","the desert","traditional knowledge","the older generation"],"k":2,"ty":"reference","e":"Preserving the knowledge.","h":"تعود على المعرفة التقليدية."},{"n":20,"s":"What is the writer's main conclusion?","o":["Satellite navigation should be banned.","Bedouin knowledge was inaccurate.","Traditional navigation knowledge is a valuable heritage worth preserving.","Only scientists can understand the stars."],"k":2,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"Stated in the final paragraph.","h":"تراث يستحق الحفظ."}]},{"t":"The gig economy","p":["The term 'gig' was originally used by musicians to describe a single performance. Today, it describes a way of working that has spread rapidly across the world: short-term, flexible jobs arranged through digital platforms. Drivers transport passengers, couriers deliver meals, freelancers design logos or translate documents, and workers complete small online tasks, all finding work through apps and websites rather than through traditional employment contracts. This is often referred to as the gig economy.","Supporters of the gig economy emphasize flexibility. Workers can, in principle, choose when, where and how much they work. This can suit students, parents with caring responsibilities, people seeking extra income alongside another job, and those who simply value independence. For people who struggle to find traditional jobs, platforms can provide a relatively easy way to start earning. Businesses also benefit, since they can hire workers only when needed, reducing costs, and consumers enjoy convenient services at competitive prices. Freelance platforms also allow skilled professionals, such as designers and programmers, to find clients in other countries without leaving home.","Critics, however, argue that this flexibility often comes at a high price for workers. Because most platforms classify workers as independent contractors rather than employees, they are generally not entitled to a minimum wage, paid holidays, sick pay or pension contributions. Income can be unpredictable, varying from week to week depending on demand. Workers usually have to pay for their own equipment, such as cars, phones and fuel, and these costs can significantly reduce their actual earnings.","Another concern is what is sometimes called 'algorithmic management'. Instead of a human manager, decisions about which jobs workers receive, how much they are paid and even whether they can continue using the platform are often made by computer algorithms. Workers may be rated by customers after every task, and low ratings can lead to fewer opportunities or deactivation, sometimes without a clear explanation or opportunity to appeal. Critics argue that this gives platforms considerable control over workers while denying them the protections normally associated with employment.","Governments and courts around the world have responded in different ways. In the United Kingdom, the Supreme Court ruled in 2021 that drivers for one major ride-hailing company should be classified as workers entitled to certain rights, such as the minimum wage and holiday pay. Other jurisdictions have created new categories of worker, or required platforms to provide insurance and greater transparency about how algorithms work. In Saudi Arabia, freelance work has been encouraged through official programmes that allow individuals to register and work legally as freelancers in many professions, as part of efforts to increase employment opportunities.","The debate over the gig economy reflects wider questions about the future of work. As technology makes it easier to divide jobs into small tasks and match them with workers instantly, more people may work in this way. The challenge for policymakers is to preserve the genuine benefits of flexibility and innovation while ensuring that workers have adequate income, security and a voice in decisions that affect their livelihoods."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the advantages and disadvantages of platform-based flexible work","how musicians organize concerts","how to start a delivery company","the history of trade unions"],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage examines the gig economy.","h":"اقتصاد العمل الحر."},{"n":22,"s":"Why does the writer mention the original meaning of 'gig'?","o":["to show that gig workers are musicians","to criticize the music industry","to explain where the term comes from","to describe a famous concert"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"It explains the origin of the word.","h":"أصل الكلمة."},{"n":23,"s":"Which group does the writer NOT mention as possibly benefiting from flexible work?","o":["students","parents with caring responsibilities","people seeking extra income","retired company directors"],"k":3,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Retired directors are not mentioned.","h":"المدراء المتقاعدون غير مذكورين."},{"n":24,"s":"Why are many gig workers not entitled to holiday pay?","o":["They work too few hours.","They are classified as independent contractors.","They refuse to take holidays.","They are paid in cash."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"تصنيفهم مستقلين."},{"n":25,"s":"The word 'unpredictable' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["very high","difficult to know in advance","fixed","illegal"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Income varies from week to week.","h":"غير متوقع."},{"n":26,"s":"What is 'algorithmic management'?","o":["training workers to use computers","managers who write software","a system for paying taxes","decisions about workers made by computer programs"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 defines it.","h":"الإدارة بالخوارزميات."},{"n":27,"s":"In paragraph 4, the word 'them' in 'denying them the protections' refers to","o":["platforms","algorithms","workers","customers"],"k":2,"ty":"reference","e":"Workers are denied protections.","h":"تعود على العمال."},{"n":28,"s":"What did the UK Supreme Court decide in 2021?","o":["Some ride-hailing drivers should be classified as workers with certain rights.","All gig platforms must close.","Drivers must pay for their own insurance.","Algorithms must be banned."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"منح السائقين حقوقًا."},{"n":29,"s":"What can be inferred about Saudi Arabia's approach to freelance work?","o":["It has banned all freelance work.","It aims to formalize freelancing and create opportunities.","It treats freelancers as company employees.","It ignores the gig economy completely."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"Official programmes allow legal registration.","h":"تنظيم العمل الحر."},{"n":30,"s":"What is the writer's overall position?","o":["Policy should keep the benefits of flexibility while protecting workers.","The gig economy should be abolished.","Workers need no protection.","Flexibility has no real benefits."],"k":0,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"The final paragraph states this.","h":"الموازنة بين المرونة والحماية."}]},{"t":"Invasive species","p":["Throughout history, humans have moved plants and animals around the world, sometimes deliberately and sometimes by accident. Most introduced species fail to survive in their new environments, and many that do survive cause few problems. A small proportion, however, spread rapidly and cause serious harm to ecosystems, economies or human health. These are known as invasive species, and they are considered one of the main threats to biodiversity worldwide.","Invasive species often succeed because they leave their natural enemies behind. In their original habitats, populations are kept in check by predators, parasites, diseases and competitors that have evolved alongside them over thousands of years. In a new environment, these controls may be absent, allowing populations to grow explosively. At the same time, native species may have no defences against the newcomers, which can outcompete them for food and space, prey on them or spread new diseases.","One of the most famous examples comes from Australia. In 1935, cane toads were introduced from South America to control beetles that were damaging sugar cane crops. The toads proved ineffective at controlling the beetles but very effective at reproducing. They spread across large areas of northern Australia, and because they are highly poisonous, native predators such as snakes and lizards that try to eat them often die. Other well-known invaders include rabbits, also in Australia, and the zebra mussel, which arrived in North America in the ballast water of ships and now blocks water pipes and damages native shellfish.","Saudi Arabia faces its own invasive species challenges. The red palm weevil, a beetle native to South and Southeast Asia, has spread through the Gulf region, probably carried in infested young palm trees. Its larvae tunnel inside the trunks of date palms, often killing them before the damage is visible from the outside. Given the cultural and economic importance of date palms, authorities have invested heavily in monitoring, quarantine measures and research to control the pest. Another example is the mesquite tree, introduced in some areas to provide shade and stabilize sand, which has spread aggressively in places and can consume large amounts of scarce groundwater.","Globalization has greatly increased the movement of species. Insects travel in shipping containers and timber, seeds attach themselves to vehicles and clothing, and marine organisms are carried across oceans in ships' ballast water and on their hulls. The trade in exotic pets and ornamental plants is another major pathway. Climate change may make matters worse by allowing some species to survive in places that were previously too cold for them.","Prevention is generally far cheaper and more effective than control. Many countries now inspect imports carefully, require ships to treat their ballast water and restrict the import of species considered high-risk. Early detection is also crucial: if an invader is discovered while its population is still small, it may be possible to eliminate it entirely. Once a species has become widely established, eradication is usually extremely difficult and expensive, and efforts must focus on limiting its spread and reducing its impact. Public awareness plays an important role, since travellers, gardeners and pet owners can all help to prevent new introductions."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how to grow date palms","the history of Australian farming","how ships are built","how invasive species spread and the harm they cause"],"k":3,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage covers invasive species.","h":"الأنواع الغازية."},{"n":32,"s":"According to the passage, why do invasive species often succeed?","o":["They are larger than native species.","They leave their natural enemies behind.","They are protected by law.","They only eat plants."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"غياب الأعداء الطبيعيين."},{"n":33,"s":"Why were cane toads introduced to Australia?","o":["to provide food for snakes","to be kept as pets","to clean water pipes","to control beetles damaging sugar cane"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"مكافحة الخنافس."},{"n":34,"s":"Why do native predators often die after eating cane toads?","o":["The toads are too large to swallow.","The toads carry a disease.","The toads live underwater.","The toads are highly poisonous."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"سامة."},{"n":35,"s":"How did zebra mussels arrive in North America?","o":["in imported timber","in the ballast water of ships","as pets","on aircraft"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 states this.","h":"مياه صابورة السفن."},{"n":36,"s":"Why is the red palm weevil particularly dangerous?","o":["It attacks only young trees in gardens.","It spreads diseases to humans.","It kills palms before the damage can be seen from outside.","It eats the fruit before harvest."],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"ضرر خفي."},{"n":37,"s":"The word 'aggressively' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["rapidly and forcefully","slowly and carefully","rarely","attractively"],"k":0,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"The tree has spread forcefully.","h":"بشراسة."},{"n":38,"s":"What can be inferred about climate change and invasive species?","o":["Climate change will remove all invasive species.","Invasive species cause climate change.","Warmer conditions may help some invaders spread to new areas.","Cold regions have no invasive species."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"قد يسهل الانتشار."},{"n":39,"s":"Why does the writer stress early detection?","o":["A small population may still be eliminated completely.","It is required by international law.","It is cheaper than inspections.","It helps invasive species survive."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 6 explains this.","h":"إمكانية القضاء المبكر."},{"n":40,"s":"Which statement best summarizes the passage?","o":["Invasive species cause serious harm, and preventing their spread is the best strategy.","Most introduced species cause great damage.","Invasive species are easy to eradicate.","Only Australia has invasive species."],"k":0,"ty":"summary / conclusion","e":"It summarizes the main points.","h":"الوقاية أفضل استراتيجية."}]}],"i":"Read each passage and answer the questions that follow. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."},{"id":"S","name":"Sentence Structure","ar":"تركيب الجمل","min":35,"from":41,"to":70,"g":[{"i":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.","err":false,"q":[{"n":41,"s":"So rapidly ______ that the authorities were unprepared.","o":["the water rose","rose the water did","the water did rise","did the water rise"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"did the water rise — So + adverb at the start: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد So."},{"n":42,"s":"Had the engineers tested the design properly, the bridge ______ still standing today.","o":["would have been","will be","would be","had been"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"would be — Mixed inverted conditional: past condition, present result.","h":"شرط مختلط مقلوب."},{"n":43,"s":"______ all the evidence, the judge reached a decision.","o":["Considered","To have considering","Being considered","Having considered"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Having considered — Perfect participle clause.","h":"Having + p.p."},{"n":44,"s":"It is imperative that the safety rules ______ at all times.","o":["are following","be followed","follow","will be follow"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"be followed — Passive subjunctive.","h":"صيغة الطلب للمجهول."},{"n":45,"s":"The painting is known ______ by a student of the famous artist.","o":["to have completed","to have been completed","to be completing","having completed"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to have been completed — Passive reporting, passive infinitive.","h":"is known to have been."},{"n":46,"s":"I wish you ______ interrupting me while I'm speaking.","o":["would stop","stopped to","will stop","have stopped"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"would stop — wish + would for irritation.","h":"wish + would."},{"n":47,"s":"It wasn't the cost ______ worried us, but the time it would take.","o":["what","that","which it","who"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"that — It wasn't … that.","h":"جملة التوكيد."},{"n":48,"s":"Not until the final chapter ______ the identity of the narrator.","o":["the reader discovers","discovers the reader","the reader does discover","does the reader discover"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"does the reader discover — Not until: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Not until."},{"n":49,"s":"The museum displays thousands of objects, the oldest of ______ dates back four thousand years.","o":["which","them","whom","that"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"which — the oldest of which.","h":"the oldest of which."},{"n":50,"s":"______ the company to relocate, many employees would lose their jobs.","o":["Had","Should","If","Were"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Were — Were + subject + to-infinitive.","h":"Were … to."},{"n":51,"s":"By the time the results were announced, the students ______ for three hours.","o":["have waited","were waiting for","waited for","had been waiting"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had been waiting — Past perfect continuous.","h":"الماضي التام المستمر."},{"n":52,"s":"The minister denied ______ about the decision in advance.","o":["having been informed","to be informed","informing","to have informed"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"having been informed — Perfect passive gerund.","h":"having been + p.p."},{"n":53,"s":"The success of the project is attributed ______ the dedication of the volunteers.","o":["for","to","on","with"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to — attribute something to.","h":"attributed to."},{"n":54,"s":"______ you finish your report today, you can take tomorrow off.","o":["Provided that","Unless","Even though","Whereas"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Provided that — Provided that = if.","h":"Provided that."},{"n":55,"s":"Supposing the flight ______ cancelled, how would you get home?","o":["is being","were","will be","has"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"were — Supposing + past (were).","h":"Supposing + were."},{"n":56,"s":"By 2030, the new railway ______ by millions of passengers.","o":["will use","has been used","will have been used","is using"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"will have been used — Future perfect passive.","h":"المستقبل التام المجهول."},{"n":57,"s":"______ he is talented, he lacks the discipline to succeed.","o":["While","Despite","In spite of","Unless"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"While — While = although.","h":"While للتضاد."},{"n":58,"s":"The committee urged the government ______ more in renewable energy.","o":["investing","invest","to invest","that investing"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to invest — urge + object + to-infinitive.","h":"urge sb to."},{"n":59,"s":"The more widely you read, ______ your writing tends to become.","o":["richer","the richest","more richer","the richer"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"the richer — Double comparative.","h":"the … the."},{"n":60,"s":"She is used ______ in front of large audiences.","o":["to speak","speaking","to speaking","for speaking"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to speaking — be used to + -ing.","h":"be used to + -ing."}]},{"i":"Choose the underlined part (A, B, C or D) that is incorrect.","err":true,"q":[{"n":61,"s":"The (A) amount of (B) students in the class (C) has (D) increased.","o":["amount","students","has","increased"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"amount — students is countable: number.","h":"number مع المعدود."},{"n":62,"s":"He (A) is (B) interested (C) to learn (D) Japanese.","o":["is","interested","to learn","Japanese"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"to learn — interested in + -ing.","h":"interested in learning."},{"n":63,"s":"The (A) report was (B) written (C) carefully by (D) she.","o":["report","written","carefully","she"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"she — Object pronoun after by: her.","h":"ضمير المفعول her."},{"n":64,"s":"Everyone (A) in the room (B) were (C) listening (D) attentively.","o":["in","were","listening","attentively"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"were — Everyone takes a singular verb: was.","h":"Everyone مفرد."},{"n":65,"s":"(A) Despite (B) to be tired, she (C) went to the (D) gym.","o":["Despite","to be","went","gym"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"to be — Despite + -ing: being.","h":"Despite + -ing."},{"n":66,"s":"If I (A) have known about the problem, I (B) would have (C) helped (D) you.","o":["have","would","helped","you"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"have — Third conditional: had known.","h":"had known."},{"n":67,"s":"The (A) children (B) enjoyed (C) theirselves at the (D) festival.","o":["children","enjoyed","theirselves","festival"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"theirselves — Reflexive pronoun: themselves.","h":"themselves."},{"n":68,"s":"(A) Although the heavy rain, the (B) match (C) continued (D) as planned.","o":["Although","match","continued","as planned"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Although — A noun phrase needs Despite.","h":"Despite مع الاسم."},{"n":69,"s":"She has (A) lived in Riyadh (B) since she (C) was a (D) children.","o":["lived","since","was","children"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"children — Singular noun: child.","h":"child مفرد."},{"n":70,"s":"The meeting (A) was (B) postponed (C) because of the manager (D) was ill.","o":["was","postponed","because of","was ill"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"because of — A clause needs because.","h":"because مع الجملة."}]}],"iar":"الأسئلة من ٤١ إلى ٦٠: اختر الكلمة أو العبارة التي تكمل الجملة إكمالًا صحيحًا."},{"id":"L","name":"Listening Comprehension","ar":"فهم المسموع","min":30,"from":71,"to":90,"g":[{"rec":1,"a":"M29-L-01","q":[{"n":71,"s":"What is the second man implying?","o":["His flatmate was too noisy at night.","He had a video call at two.","He wants to visit Canada.","He did not hear anything."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"He describes hearing every word.","h":"تلميح للإزعاج."}],"t":"A hint about a noisy flatmate","sc":["Situation: Two flatmates are talking in the morning. (65 words)","Man: Did you sleep well last night?","Man: Not really. Someone seemed to be having a very lively video call at two in the morning. I could hear every word through the wall.","Man: Ah. That was me, talking to my cousin in Canada. I didn't realize the walls were so thin.","Man: No problem, but perhaps you could use headphones or take late calls in the living room."]},{"rec":2,"a":"M29-L-02","q":[{"n":72,"s":"What is the professor warning the student about?","o":["He has failed the course.","He must change his transport.","He may not be allowed to take the final exam.","He must change his seat."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"He mentions the attendance policy.","h":"تحذير من الحرمان."}],"t":"A professor's indirect warning about attendance","sc":["Situation: A professor speaks to a student after class. (61 words)","Man: Rakan, I've noticed your seat has been empty quite often recently.","Man: Yes, I've had some problems with transport.","Man: I understand. You may want to check the attendance policy in the course guide. Students who miss more than twenty-five per cent of classes can't sit the final exam.","Man: I didn't know that. I'll make sure I attend every class from now on."]},{"rec":3,"a":"M29-L-03","q":[{"n":73,"s":"What is the second woman's first reaction?","o":["She is sarcastic about the late report.","She is genuinely impressed.","She is worried about her own report.","She is angry with the professor."],"k":0,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"'That must be a new record' is sarcastic.","h":"سخرية."}],"t":"A friend's sarcastic congratulations","sc":["Situation: Two friends are talking. (54 words)","Woman: I've finally finished my report. Only three weeks after the deadline.","Woman: Well, congratulations. That must be a new record.","Woman: Very funny. I know it's late, but my professor gave me an extension because I was ill.","Woman: Oh, I'm sorry, I didn't know you'd been ill. I take it back. Well done for finishing it."]},{"rec":4,"a":"M29-L-04","q":[{"n":74,"s":"What does the second man's offer suggest?","o":["He wants to give the presentation himself.","He has not seen the slides.","He thinks the slides are perfect.","He thinks the slides need improvement."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"He mentions unclear charts and typos.","h":"نقد ضمني."}],"t":"An offer to help that hides criticism","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are talking about a presentation. (65 words)","Man: I've finished the slides for tomorrow's presentation to the clients.","Man: Great. Would you like me to go through them with you? I could help make some of the charts a bit clearer, and perhaps check the spelling.","Man: Are they that bad?","Man: Not at all, but a few of the numbers are hard to read, and I noticed a couple of typos on the first slide."]},{"rec":5,"a":"M29-L-05","q":[{"n":75,"s":"How confident is the woman about the deadline?","o":["She is completely confident.","She knows it is impossible.","She has not started the work.","She is uncertain."],"k":3,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"'Well … it should be. Probably.'","h":"غير متأكدة."}],"t":"A hesitant answer about a deadline","sc":["Situation: A manager asks an employee about a project. (56 words)","Woman: Will the website be ready for launch on Sunday?","Woman: Well … it should be. Probably. Most of the pages are finished, but we're still waiting for the photographs, and there may be some problems with the payment system.","Woman: So there's a risk it won't be ready?","Woman: Yes, I'm afraid there is. I'll know for certain by Thursday."]},{"rec":6,"a":"M29-L-06","q":[{"n":76,"s":"What does the second man's answer mean?","o":["He will definitely attend.","He is not sure whether he will attend.","He refuses to attend.","He will attend with his father."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"'Let's say it's a maybe.'","h":"غير مؤكد."}],"t":"A vague promise to attend","sc":["Situation: Two friends are talking about a party. (55 words)","Man: Are you coming to my brother's graduation party on Friday?","Man: I'll try my best. I might have to help my father at the shop, but if I finish early, I'll definitely come.","Man: So is that a yes or a no?","Man: Let's say it's a maybe. I'll message you on Friday afternoon when I know more."]},{"rec":7,"a":"M29-L-07","q":[{"n":77,"s":"What is the first woman really complaining about?","o":["She wants a holiday.","The coffee is too expensive.","The coffee machine breaks down too often.","The office manager is rude."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"Her joke hides a real complaint.","h":"تعطل الآلة المتكرر."}],"t":"A complaint framed as a joke","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are talking in the office kitchen. (60 words)","Woman: I see the coffee machine is broken again. Maybe we should just give it a holiday. It works one day a week, after all.","Woman: Ha! It does seem to break down more than it works.","Woman: Seriously, though, it's the third time this month. We should ask the office manager to replace it.","Woman: I'll mention it at the staff meeting tomorrow."]},{"rec":8,"a":"M29-L-08","q":[{"n":78,"s":"Why does the man change the plan?","o":["The café is closed.","He is feeling ill.","His uncle has arrived unexpectedly.","He has to work late. Longer conversations"],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"His family is having a dinner for his uncle.","h":"وصول عمه المفاجئ."}],"t":"An unexpected change of plans","sc":["Situation: Two friends are talking on the phone. (54 words)","Man: Are we still meeting at the café at six?","Man: Actually, there's been a change of plan. My uncle has arrived unexpectedly from Madinah, and my family's having a dinner for him tonight.","Man: Oh, no problem. Shall we meet tomorrow instead?","Man: Tomorrow evening would be perfect. Same place, same time. Sorry about the short notice."]},{"rec":9,"a":"M29-L-09","q":[{"n":79,"s":"What was the main cause of the incident?","o":["The student used an ordinary beaker instead of heat-resistant glass.","The hot plate was broken.","The student was not wearing goggles.","The solution was poisonous."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"'Ordinary glass can't withstand rapid heating.'","h":"استخدام كأس عادي."},{"n":80,"s":"What will the supervisor suggest?","o":["banning students from the lab","labelling heat-resistant beakers more clearly and buying more","removing all hot plates","giving the student a lower grade"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Stated at the end.","h":"تمييز الأكواب وشراء المزيد."}],"t":"Discussing a lab safety incident","sc":["Situation: A laboratory supervisor speaks to a student. (187 words)","Woman: Thank you for coming. I need to talk to you about what happened in the lab yesterday.","Man: You mean when the beaker cracked?","Woman: Yes. Fortunately, nobody was hurt, but I need to understand exactly what happened for the incident report.","Man: I was heating a solution on the hot plate. I think I turned the temperature up too high, and the glass cracked.","Woman: Was it the heat-resistant glass we keep on the left shelf?","Man: No, I took an ordinary beaker because the others were being used. I didn't think it would make a difference.","Woman: That's the main problem. Ordinary glass can't withstand rapid heating. Were you wearing your safety goggles?","Man: Yes, and gloves.","Woman: Good. That's probably why you weren't injured. From now on, if the right equipment isn't available, please wait or ask a technician. Never substitute equipment without checking.","Man: I understand. I'm sorry.","Woman: Mistakes help us improve. I'll suggest that we label the heat-resistant beakers more clearly and buy a few more.","Man: Should I write a statement for the report?","Woman: Yes, please. Just describe what happened in your own words and email it to me by tomorrow."]},{"rec":10,"a":"M29-L-10","q":[{"n":81,"s":"Why does the first woman want international students to share their own customs?","o":["to save time for the organizers","because Saudi students refuse to speak","to test their English","to make the workshop a two-way exchange"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"'it becomes a genuine exchange'.","h":"تبادل حقيقي."},{"n":82,"s":"Who will the second woman contact about the budget?","o":["the professors","the student affairs office","a coffee shop","the international students"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Stated in the conversation.","h":"شؤون الطلاب."}],"t":"Planning a cross-cultural workshop","sc":["Situation: Two students are planning a workshop for international students. (171 words)","Woman: We're running a workshop for new international students next month. The aim is to help them understand Saudi culture and university life.","Woman: I think we should start with something practical, like greetings, hospitality customs and how to address professors.","Woman: Good. Many students feel nervous about making mistakes, so we should make it clear that people here are generally very understanding.","Woman: We should also invite Saudi students to share their experiences, not just give a lecture. Small group discussions would be more engaging.","Woman: And the international students could share customs from their own countries. Then it becomes a genuine exchange rather than a one-way presentation.","Woman: I like that. What about a cultural activity at the end? We could serve Arabic coffee and dates and explain the traditions around them.","Woman: Perfect. We need to check the budget for refreshments, though.","Woman: I'll contact the student affairs office tomorrow. They usually support events like this.","Woman: And let's prepare a short guide they can take away, with useful phrases in Arabic and tips about campus services."]},{"rec":11,"a":"M29-L-11","q":[{"n":83,"s":"What was the main problem with the sign-up process?","o":["It did not work on some phones.","It required too much information.","It was only in English.","It sent too many reminders."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Eight fields were required.","h":"طويل جدًا."},{"n":84,"s":"Why do they suggest adding a tutorial?","o":["The reminders were confusing.","The colours were too bright.","Some users did not notice the calendar feature.","Users forgot their passwords. Talks and lectures"],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"Some didn't notice it.","h":"إبراز الميزات."}],"t":"Evaluating a mobile app prototype","sc":["Situation: Three students are testing an app they designed. (185 words)","Hamad: We've tested our study-planner app with twenty students. Let's go through the feedback.","Sultan: Overall, people liked the idea. Most said the reminders were really useful.","Rayan: But many found the sign-up process too long. They had to fill in eight different fields before they could even use the app.","Hamad: That's a problem. If people give up before they start, nothing else matters. Could we reduce it to just name, email and password?","Sultan: Yes. We can ask for other information later, once they're using the app.","Rayan: Another comment was that the colours made some text hard to read, especially in bright sunlight.","Hamad: We should increase the contrast. And did anyone mention the calendar feature?","Sultan: Only a few people used it. Some didn't even notice it was there.","Rayan: Maybe we need a short tutorial when the app opens for the first time, showing the main features.","Hamad: Good idea. Let's make these changes and test again with a new group next week.","Sultan: Should we use the same questionnaire, so we can compare the results?","Rayan: Yes, but let's add a question about the tutorial, to see whether it actually helps."]},{"rec":12,"a":"M29-L-12","q":[{"n":85,"s":"According to the lecturer, why was space exploration so expensive in the past?","o":["Astronauts were paid very high salaries.","Satellites were very large.","Fuel was unavailable.","Rockets could only be used once."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"The comparison with aircraft explains this.","h":"الصواريخ تُستخدم مرة واحدة."},{"n":86,"s":"Why does the lecturer compare rockets with aircraft?","o":["to explain how planes fly","to show how wasteful single-use rockets were","to argue that planes are dangerous","to describe a new type of aircraft"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"Imagine throwing away aircraft after one flight.","h":"توضيح الهدر."},{"n":87,"s":"Where does much of the value of the space economy come from?","o":["tourism on the Moon","mining on Mars","services used on Earth, such as communications and navigation","selling rockets to museums"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"الخدمات على الأرض."}],"t":"Extended lecture: the economics of space exploration","sc":["Situation: An economics lecturer speaks to students. (300 words)","Lecturer: Good morning. Today, we'll look at space exploration from an economic perspective. For most of the twentieth century, space activities were dominated by governments, especially the United States and the Soviet Union, and they were driven largely by national prestige and military competition. The Apollo programme, which landed astronauts on the Moon, cost the equivalent of hundreds of billions of today's dollars. One of the main reasons space has been so expensive is the cost of launching objects into orbit. For decades, rockets were used only once: after a launch, they fell into the ocean or burned up in the atmosphere. Imagine how expensive air travel would be if every aircraft were thrown away after a single flight. In recent years, private companies have developed reusable rockets that land back on Earth and can be flown again. Together with increased competition, this has significantly reduced the cost of launching each kilogram into orbit. Lower costs have transformed the economics of space. Thousands of small satellites now provide services such as internet access, weather forecasting, navigation and Earth observation for agriculture and disaster response. The global space economy is now worth several hundred billion dollars a year, and much of that value comes from services used on Earth rather than from exploration itself. Many countries, including Saudi Arabia, which established a national space agency and sent astronauts to the International Space Station in 2023, see the space sector as an opportunity to develop technology, skills and new industries. However, economists also raise concerns. The growing number of satellites increases the risk of collisions and space debris, which could make some orbits unusable. And questions remain about whether ambitious projects such as human missions to Mars can ever be financially justified, or whether their value lies mainly in scientific knowledge and inspiration."]},{"rec":13,"a":"M29-L-13","q":[{"n":88,"s":"How did the earliest writing in Mesopotamia develop?","o":["from clay tokens used to record goods","from pictures painted in caves","from Egyptian hieroglyphs","from the Phoenician alphabet"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"من رموز طينية للمحاسبة."},{"n":89,"s":"What is the rebus principle?","o":["using signs to represent sounds rather than meanings","writing from right to left","using one sign for every word","carving letters into stone"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Explained with the 'bee-leaf' example.","h":"تمثيل الأصوات."},{"n":90,"s":"Why did the alphabet make writing easier to learn?","o":["It used thousands of pictures.","It was written only on clay.","It was used only by scribes.","It used a small number of letters for sounds."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"عدد قليل من الحروف."}],"t":"Lecture: the evolution of writing systems","sc":["Situation: A linguistics lecturer speaks to students. (297 words)","Lecturer: Good afternoon. Writing is one of humanity's most important inventions, yet compared with spoken language, it is very recent. Humans have probably been speaking for well over a hundred thousand years, but the earliest true writing appeared only about five thousand years ago. Writing seems to have been invented independently in at least three or four places: Mesopotamia, Egypt, China and Mesoamerica. In Mesopotamia, the earliest writing developed from a system of clay tokens used to keep accounts of goods such as grain and sheep. Gradually, these marks evolved into a script called cuneiform, written by pressing a reed into wet clay. Early writing systems were largely logographic, meaning that each sign represented a word or idea. This required learning hundreds or even thousands of signs, so writing was limited to specially trained scribes. Over time, however, scribes began to use some signs to represent sounds rather than meanings, an approach known as the rebus principle, similar to drawing a picture of a bee and a leaf to represent the word 'belief'. This led to syllabic systems, in which signs represent syllables. The most important development for many modern scripts was the alphabet, which emerged in the eastern Mediterranean around 1800 to 1500 BCE. By representing individual consonant sounds with a small number of letters, it made writing much easier to learn. The Phoenician alphabet influenced Greek, Latin and Aramaic, and Aramaic in turn gave rise to scripts including Arabic. Arabic itself developed its distinctive cursive form and, after the coming of Islam, became one of the most widely used scripts in the world, valued not only for communication but also as an art form in calligraphy. Today, the digital age raises new questions about writing, from emoji to the encoding of thousands of scripts on computers."]}],"i":"You will hear 13 recordings: 8 short conversations (one question each), 3 longer conversations (two questions each) and 2 talks (three questions each). Read the questions before each recording, then choose the best answer.","iar":"ستسمع ثلاثة عشر تسجيلًا: ثماني محادثات قصيرة (سؤال لكل منها)، وثلاث محادثات أطول (سؤالان لكل منها)، وحديثين أو محاضرتين (ثلاثة أسئلة لكل منهما). اقرأ الأسئلة قبل بدء كل تسجيل."},{"id":"C","name":"Composition Analysis","ar":"التحليل الإنشائي","min":20,"from":91,"to":100,"g":[{"q":[{"n":91,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Musical instruments can be expensive.","Learning to play a musical instrument has many benefits.","Some people prefer listening to music.","Concerts are held in large halls."],"x":["______ It can improve memory, reduce stress and even help people make friends. Playing in a group also teaches cooperation and listening skills."],"k":1,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details list benefits of playing music.","h":"فوائد العزف."},{"n":92,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Some houses are painted white.","Windows can be made of glass.","These features help to keep the heat out during the day.","Modern houses are often large."],"x":["Many traditional houses in hot regions have small windows and thick walls. ______ This design kept homes comfortable long before air conditioning was invented."],"k":2,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"It explains the purpose before the conclusion.","h":"تفسير الغرض."},{"n":93,"s":"The (A) informations provided (B) by the guide (C) was (D) very useful.","o":["informations","by","was","very"],"k":0,"ty":"error","e":"information is uncountable.","h":"information غير معدود."},{"n":94,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Individuals can help protect the environment through everyday actions.","Public transport is cheap.","Food waste is a global problem.","Lights use electricity."],"x":["______ Small changes, such as switching off lights, using public transport and reducing food waste, can make a significant difference when millions of people adopt them."],"k":0,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details give everyday actions.","h":"أفعال يومية لحماية البيئة."},{"n":95,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Many apps are free to download.","However, spending too much time online can affect sleep, concentration and mood.","Some people have several accounts.","Phones have become thinner."],"x":["Social media allows people to stay in touch with friends and family around the world. ______ For this reason, experts recommend setting limits on daily screen time."],"k":1,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"A problem is needed before the recommendation.","h":"مشكلة قبل التوصية."},{"n":96,"s":"She (A) has (B) been working (C) at the hospital (D) since ten years.","o":["has","been","at","since"],"k":3,"ty":"error","e":"Duration: for ten years.","h":"for مع المدة."},{"n":97,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4","Sentence 5"],"x":["(1) The Empty Quarter is one of the largest sand deserts in the world. (2) It covers parts of Saudi Arabia, Oman, the UAE and Yemen. (3) Its sand dunes can reach heights of over two hundred metres. (4) Riyadh has many modern skyscrapers. (5) Despite the harsh conditions, some plants and animals survive there."],"k":2,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Skyscrapers in Riyadh do not develop the point about the desert.","h":"ناطحات السحاب خارج الفكرة."},{"n":98,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["2 – 1 – 3 – 4","3 – 2 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 4 – 1"],"x":["(1) Then, he practised speaking every day with a language partner.","(2) Abdullah decided to improve his English before applying to university.","(3) First, he enrolled in an evening course at a language centre.","(4) After six months, he passed the exam with an excellent score."],"k":2,"ty":"best order","e":"decision → First → Then → result.","h":"قرار ثم خطوات ثم نتيجة."},{"n":99,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Bicycles have two wheels.","These changes could make cities healthier and more pleasant places to live.","Some roads are very wide.","Scooters can be rented online."],"x":["Electric scooters, bicycles and walking paths are becoming more common in Saudi cities. They reduce traffic, lower pollution and encourage people to be more active. ______"],"k":1,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"It sums up the benefits.","h":"خلاصة الفوائد."},{"n":100,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["As a result","In addition","For example","On the other hand Teacher Section — Mock 29"],"x":["The hotel was close to the beach and had excellent service. ______, the rooms were small and rather noisy."],"k":3,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"The second sentence contrasts with the first.","h":"On the other hand للمقابلة."}]}],"i":"Read each paragraph or set of sentences and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل فقرة أو مجموعة جمل واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."}]};
